00:08:48.824 --> 00:08:53.984
The analysis is adjusting for sector rotation overwhelming single-name catalysts, which is

00:08:53.984 --> 00:08:57.404
why the volume filter now sits in front of the next catalyst entry.

00:08:57.864 --> 00:09:04.384
Keeping 40% of a TFSA in TSX listed financials and energy names because the companies feel

00:09:04.384 --> 00:09:09.964
familiar is not diversification. It is doubling down on the same economic exposures already

00:09:09.964 --> 00:09:15.244
sitting in a Canadian job and a Canadian house. Motley Fool Canada put the mechanism in

00:09:15.244 --> 00:09:21.824
plain weights. Canada is roughly 3% of global equity market capitalization. Yet many portfolios

00:09:21.824 --> 00:09:27.904
hold 30% to 50% in Canadian stocks. That overweight lands in two sectors that together make up

00:09:27.904 --> 00:09:33.544
about half the T.S.X. So the homebook is a financials and energy book with a maple leaf label.

00:09:33.544 --> 00:09:39.864
The S&amp;P 500 hitting a new high while the TSX lingers below its August peak is the performance

00:09:39.864 --> 00:09:41.784
gap that overweight has to justify.

00:09:42.224 --> 00:09:47.284
Run a geographic allocation report in the brokerage once a quarter and compare the Canadian

00:09:47.284 --> 00:09:52.844
weight against the MSCI World X Canada benchmark, which is the unglamorous habit that makes

00:09:52.844 --> 00:09:53.944
the overweight visible.

00:09:54.504 --> 00:09:59.204
The common misconception is that familiarity with domestic names automatically justifies

00:09:59.204 --> 00:10:00.004
that concentration.

00:10:00.004 --> 00:10:06.144
The fix is to treat home bias as an explicit size decision rather than the default setting

00:10:06.144 --> 00:10:07.184
of a TFSA.

00:10:07.744 --> 00:10:11.124
Size it on purpose if you want the dividend stream and the energy tape.

00:10:11.564 --> 00:10:14.924
Do not let it size itself because the tickers are the ones you already know.

